  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#consume-a-character-reference\n\nIn double-quoted attribute value, `&\"` is not a parse error.\n\nIn single-quoted attribute value, `&'` is not a parse error.\n\nUn unquoted attribute value, `&>` is not a parse error.\n\nIn other cases, they are parse errors.\n\nFWIW, in HTML4, none of those were errors.\n\n```\n<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//EN\" \"http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd\">\n<title>x</title>\n<p>&> &\" &' &</p>\n```\n\nhttp://validator.w3.org/#validate_by_input\n\nI see little benefit of the current setup over just allowing `\"'>` to appear after a `&`, i.e. add those codepoints to the top of the \"consume a character reference\" state machine, and change the syntax section accordingly.\n",
